```
ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: green
MANDATORY_BASELINES: [{"name": "Dense Retriever Similarity", "why": "Standard baseline for tool matching/retrieval in ToolBench/API-Bank; captures semantic similarity far better than lexical.", "repo_or_cite": "ToolLLM / API-Bank"}, {"name": "BM25 Schema Overlap", "why": "Standard sparse retrieval baseline to ensure simple lexical overlap isn't artificially handicapped.", "repo_or_cite": "Standard IR"}, {"name": "LLM-as-a-judge confusability", "why": "Predicting confusability directly via an LLM meta-prompt is the standard baseline alternative to geometric heuristics.", "repo_or_cite": "LLM-as-a-Judge (Zheng et al.)"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Using lexical/hashed bag-of-words in L0 is a massive strawman for 'schema geometry' and is fully expected to fail at capturing latent semantic confusability.", "Treating 'difficulty' and 'confusability' as perfectly orthogonal synthetic latents ignores that in real toolsets, difficulty is often causally downstream of semantic overlap."]   MISSING_METRICS: ["Precision-Recall AUC (PR-AUC) for imbalanced error classes", "Expected Calibration Error (ECE) or Brier Score to ensure the baseline logprobs are genuinely calibrated"]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Evaluate on the Berkeley Function Calling Leaderboard (BFCL) or ToolBench using real dense embeddings (e.g., sentence-transformers) for schema similarity, comparing PR-AUC of the schema feature against rigorously calibrated model logprobs (with known ECE) for predicting actual wrong-tool-selection errors.
RATIONALE: The L0 evaluation correctly killed the claim, successfully executing the anti-relabeling test and finding that schema geometry is dominated by difficulty/logprob. I vote green to accept this clean negative result. However, the L0 test relied on a lexical strawman. Any future L1 attempt must use real dense embeddings against real calibrated logprobs on established benchmarks (BFCL) with PR-AUC, as ROC-AUC is misleading for minority-class error prediction.
